WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.811 --> 00:00:04.592
- How do you create a part of a person

00:00:04.592 --> 00:00:07.430
that feels like it is a part of them?

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.910
So that they can not just like get by,

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.227
but so they can express their self

00:00:11.227 --> 00:00:12.609
with the limb that they have,

00:00:12.609 --> 00:00:15.699
they can feel good about it,
they can feel sexy, inspired,

00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:19.276
and most importantly shows
that having a limb difference

00:00:19.276 --> 00:00:23.055
is not something that
necessarily stops you.

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:25.676
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:25.676 --> 00:00:29.476
My name is Seth Kane, also
known as Dr. Adventure,

00:00:29.476 --> 00:00:32.906
and I am the founder and CEO
of The New Flesh Workshop,

00:00:32.906 --> 00:00:34.626
but most of my friends just call me Doc.

00:00:34.626 --> 00:00:35.459
- [Interviewer] Just to clarify,

00:00:35.459 --> 00:00:36.292
you're not actually a doctor?

00:00:36.292 --> 00:00:40.173
- I am not actually a physician or a PhD.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:43.973
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:43.973 --> 00:00:47.706
The New Flesh Workshop is
something I started in 2007

00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:49.376
just in my mom's garage.

00:00:49.376 --> 00:00:51.706
What we do is develop various types

00:00:51.706 --> 00:00:54.935
of prosthetic add-ons and customizations

00:00:54.935 --> 00:00:57.624
by medical implant
devices and various types

00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:00.745
of wearable technologies
for human enhancement.

00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:03.306
There's been several firsts that have

00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:05.397
come out of The New Flesh Workshop.

00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:07.925
As far as I know we're the first company

00:01:07.925 --> 00:01:11.496
to make a ballistic case
mod for a prosthetic leg.

00:01:11.496 --> 00:01:13.296
That's a cool piece, we
can make a prosthetic

00:01:13.296 --> 00:01:16.155
that'll stop nine millimeter rounds.

00:01:16.155 --> 00:01:18.336
We've created for, you know, a couple hundred dollars

00:01:18.336 --> 00:01:22.665
a DIY solution for missing
your ear on the outside

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:24.826
and still being able
to hear without having

00:01:24.826 --> 00:01:27.285
to go though multiple major surgeries

00:01:27.285 --> 00:01:30.535
and having holes drilled in your skull.

00:01:33.696 --> 00:01:35.597
Do I consider myself a mad scientist?

00:01:35.597 --> 00:01:38.666
I consider myself a fairly
happy scientist actually.

00:01:38.666 --> 00:01:42.216
A lot of my inspiration
comes from stories,

00:01:42.216 --> 00:01:44.745
comic books and sci-fi.

00:01:44.745 --> 00:01:46.245
The writers of these kinds of stories

00:01:46.245 --> 00:01:50.056
are always looking at
what could happen next.

00:01:50.056 --> 00:01:51.514
But they don't know really how to do it

00:01:51.514 --> 00:01:52.754
but I think at some
point somebody has to be like,

00:01:52.754 --> 00:01:54.602
"No, no, I'm gonna like,
Jules Verne was right

00:01:54.602 --> 00:01:55.602
"I'm gonna build a submarine."

00:01:55.602 --> 00:01:57.221
Somebody's gotta do that

00:01:57.221 --> 00:01:59.672
and that is probably the thing that really

00:01:59.672 --> 00:02:01.394
gets me up in the morning with a smile on

00:02:01.394 --> 00:02:04.144
to go and make something happen.

